\ LOCALS.SEQ    Local variables for F88         by Tom Zimmer
\                                                       F-PC native port

ANEW LOCALVARS

comment:

        Adds LOCAL variables to F88, usage is as follows:

        : localswap     ( n1 n2 --- n2 n1 )
                        2 locals                \ we need 2 local variables
                        locala ! localb !
                        locala @ localb @ ;

        Local variables LOCALA through LOCALD are provided. Local
        variables are CELL in size, if you want to use a double word
        local variable, use LOCALB or LOCALD, and specify 2 or 4 LOCALS

        Local variables are automatically deallocated at the end of the
        definition. You can mix the use of local variables with the
        return stack operators, but be aware that local variabls place
        an extra function on the return stack to clean up the local
        variables on definition exit, so you CANNOT do things like
        " R> DROP EXIT" to pop up one level..This would be bad practice
        in any case since F88 puts two things on the return stack for
        each nest level.

        Native port: cells are 32 bits and a return stack frame is one
        cell (the IP), so LOCALS is now a colon definition that slips the
        IP of 0LOCALS's (0LOCALS) under its own return address. The
        local stack holds n cells and then a count cell, the number of
        bytes to release. The CODE words are rewritten in Forth.

comment;

create lstack 512 cells allot   \ local variable stack

variable lstkptr                \ local stack pointer
  lstack lstkptr !

: (0locals)     ( --- )         \ release the newest group of locals
                lstkptr @ @ negate lstkptr +! ;

: 0locals       ( --- )
                exit            \ protect against accidental execution
                (0locals)
                ;

: locals        ( n -- )        \ allocate n cell locals until the end of
                                \ the calling definition
                1+ cells dup lstkptr +!         \ n cells + the count cell
                lstkptr @ lstack 512 cells + u>
                abort" Out of local variable space"
                lstkptr @ !                     \ save count in local stack
                r>                              \ our return into the caller
                [ ' 0locals >body @ cell+ ] literal >r  \ caller exits into
                >r ;                            \ (0LOCALS), then returns

: locala        ( --- a1 )      lstkptr @ 1 cells - ;

: localb        ( --- a1 )      lstkptr @ 2 cells - ;

: localc        ( --- a1 )      lstkptr @ 3 cells - ;

: locald        ( --- a1 )      lstkptr @ 4 cells - ;

\s      Sample usage of local variables.

: findXinString ( string --- n1 )     \ Search for the first occurance
                1 locals locala on    \ of the "x" character in string
                bl word count 0       \ following tt, return n1 offset to "x".
               ?do      dup i + c@ ascii x =
                        if      i locala ! leave
                        then
                loop    drop locala @ ;


